Grove PATCH-32: let the Particl wallet be born at the tip (PART_WALLET_SCAN_FROM)

PATCH-31 made the Particl conf generatable as pruned, which is what a snapshot
restore needs on the CHAIN side. This is the WALLET side of the same feature, and
without it a restored snapshot cannot be started at all.

`extkeyimportmaster`'s sixth positional argument is scan_chain_from -- "scan for
transactions in blocks after timestamp, negative number to skip" -- and
particl-core acts on it as `if (nScanFrom >= 0) { RescanFromTime(...) }`
(rpchdwallet.cpp:1850). Upstream never passes it, so it takes its default of 1:
scan from the beginning of time, i.e. the entire chain.

On an empty datadir that costs nothing, which is why it has never mattered. On a
datadir that already holds a chain it is fatal, and in both possible orderings.
Measured 2026-09-09 against a real prepare and a real engine:

  restore snapshot, then prepare
      The new wallet rescans the whole retained window. Measured
      "Rescan completed in 151741ms" for 475,213 blocks, against the engine's
      10-second RPC client timeout (basicswap/rpc.py:33), so prepare dies with

          ValueError: RPC server error: timed out, method: extkeyimportmaster

      The rescan itself SUCCEEDS and the wallet is correct; only prepare's exit
      is wrong. That is worse than a clean failure, not better -- it leaves a
      usable datadir behind a reported error.

  prepare, then restore snapshot
      The wallet is created against an empty chain, so its last-synced height is
      0. Dropping in a chain pruned to height 1,764,346 puts that below
      pruneheight and particld refuses to start:

          Error: Prune: last wallet synchronisation goes beyond pruned data.
          You need to -reindex (download the whole blockchain again in case of
          pruned node)

There is no third ordering. The wallet must be created while the chain is
already at the tip, AND it must not try to walk back through it.

A freshly derived wallet has no history to find -- it is a BIP-85 child minted
seconds earlier -- so the scan is pure waste in exactly the case that breaks.
Passing -1 skips it and the wallet is born at whatever tip the node is on.

    PART_WALLET_SCAN_FROM=<unix ts>   scan for transactions after that time
                          =-1        skip the scan; wallet starts at the tip
                          unset      upstream behaviour, byte-identical

Absent the variable the original single-argument call is used unchanged, so a
runtime carrying this patch behaves exactly as upstream until something asks
otherwise. The other five arguments appear only because the parameter is
positional, and each is upstream's own default restated: passphrase "",
save_bip44_root false, master_label "Master Key", account_label "Default
Account" (rpchdwallet.cpp:1652-1668). None of them changes.

Deliberately NOT defaulted to -1 inside the engine. Skipping the scan is right
for a wallet this process just derived and wrong for a wallet being RESTORED
from a user's own phrase with history to recover -- and prepare cannot tell those
apart from in here. The caller knows which it is doing, so the caller decides.
On a pruned node the restore case cannot be served by a scan anyway: the blocks
below pruneheight are gone, so a full recovery needs an unpruned node regardless.

